<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zubaidah bint Ja'far</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 831</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Zubayda, Umm Ja'far, Amat al-'Aziz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zubaidah bint Ja'far was an Abbasid princess, a granddaughter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mansur and the wife and double cousin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a>, whom she married in 781–82; their son became the caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amin">al-Amin</a>. She is remembered above all for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> and pools she built for Muslim pilgrims on the route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, renamed the Darb Zubaidah in her honour. After a drought she had the Zamzam Well deepened and spent over 2 million dinars on the water supply of Mecca, including an aqueduct from the spring of Hunayn, 95 kilometres to the east. She also rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> after an earthquake in 791. Her son was killed by supporters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a> in 813, and she died in 831.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Abbasid princes married within the family, as Harun al-Rashid married his cousin <b>Zubayda</b>, a granddaughter of al-Mansur, and they governed provinces: Harun’s succession plan of 802 gave al-Ma’mun Khurasan, and al-Mu’tasim governed Egypt and Syria for his brother.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=821" aria-label="Baghdad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5684 954 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Zubaidah bint Ja'far"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6134 1224h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=821">What was here in 821 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
